   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to death certificates",
    "basePrintNo": "S6815",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6815-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4174, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6815\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to death certificates\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo expand the list of people authorized to request and receive either a\ncertified copy or a certified transcript of a NYS death certificate.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Section 4174 of the Public Health Law, as it relates to\nrecords; transcripts, and certification by commissioner; fees, to clari-\nfy that the following additional individuals are legally eligible to\nrequest and receive a certified copy of a NYS death certificate: an\n\"agent\" as designated pursuant to subdivision 3 of section 4201 of PHL,\nand a domestic partner, grandparents, grandchildren, aunts, uncles,\ncousins, nieces or nephews of the deceased.\n\nSection 2 sets an effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nExcept for New York City, which has its own vital records system, certi-\nfied copies of NYS death certificates may be obtained from either the\nBureau of Vital Records of the NYS Department of Health (DOH) or from a\nlocal registrar of the municipality where a death occurred. State law\ncurrently provides that the only individuals eligible to get a copy of a\nNYS death certificate are the spouse, parent, child, or sibling of the\ndeceased or their lawful representative. Any other individual - even if\nrelated to the deceased - must document a \"lawful right or claim\", a\nmedical need, or possess a New York State court order.\n\nSuch restrictions place an undue burden on a deceased's family member,\nwho would otherwise be eligible to obtain a copy of a death certificate,\nby putting them through unnecessary administrative burdens at a time\nwhen they are already undergoing the loss of a relative. The limited\neligibility list also creates awkwardness for both family members as\nwell as funeral directors, who are frequently charged with securing\ncopies of. death certificates for family members which they routinely\nneed for estate, benefit, and/or insurance purposes. Finally, New York\nCity's list of individuals who may request a death certificate is much\nmore expansive and up-to-date, allowing nine individuals to request a\ndeath certificate and eight more under more limited circumstances.\n\nThis legislation would eliminate the aforementioned obstacles and\nburdens, more readily clarify in statute who is eligible and facilitate\nwhat can be an arduous process for family and funeral directors alike.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\n\nCould result in additional revenue via processing fees to NYS Vital\nRecords and/or local government registrar's offices.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nEffective immediately.\n",
